{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"mega-glaciered-mongrel","creation_channel":"cli","description":"# The inbound absence-probe port is DORMANT after 3b5f — decide: keep or delete\n\nBug `3b5f-1b21-ee14-4bdf` removed the dead `(inbound, probe)` chain (the producer\n`differ._compute_mutations_emit_absent_partner_probes`, the dispatch arm\n`_run_differs_inbound_probe_dispatch`, and `reconcile_helpers.route_inbound_probe`). It\ndeliberately did NOT remove the port capability those consumed:\n\n* `_backend.SupportsAbsenceProbe` / `backend.probe_remote`\n* `inbound_probe.py` (`ProbeBranch`, `ProbeResult`, `ProbeConfigError`)\n* the two adapter implementations: Cloud `adapters/jira/probe.py`, DC\n  `adapters/jira_datacenter/transport.py` `probe_remote`\n\n## Why 3b5f excluded it (a reasoned exclusion, not an oversight)\n\nRemoving DC's `probe_remote` removes DC's ONLY import of the shared\n`classify_probe_response`, leaving it used by Cloud alone — which weakens **epic e369's\nAC5**, whose claim is that the probe classifier has one implementation in `jira_family`\nimported by BOTH backends. Trading an epic-level AC to delete a dormant Protocol is a\nseparate decision, so 3b5f recorded the capability as dormant (in `inbound_probe.py`'s\ndocstring, `SupportsAbsenceProbe`'s docstring, and ADR 0028's corrected Consequences)\nand filed this.\n\n## The decision\n\nEither:\n1. **Delete** the port and both adapter implementations, and re-establish e369 AC5's\n   both-backends claim on `classify_probe_response` some other way (or amend AC5); or\n2. **Keep** it and give it a live consumer — the natural home is `binding_walk`, which\n   already performs the bounded GET and already knows `CONFIRMED_404`, and which\n   currently reaches into another module's private `outbound_differ._safe_get_issue`\n   (a real layering violation the port would fix).\n\nNote option 2 must NOT be used to restore same-pass RE-CREATION: the operator ruling on\n3b5f is that a confirmed-deleted issue is never resurrected. Any consumer must route a\nconfirmed 404 to the tombstone, not to a create.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] a decision is recorded (delete vs re-home) with the e369 AC5 consequence stated\n- [ ] if delete: `classify_probe_response`'s both-backends claim is either preserved by\n      another importer or e369 AC5 is amended, with the amendment linked\n- [ ] if re-home: the new consumer routes a confirmed 404 to the tombstone, never to a\n      re-create, and a RED-first test proves the port is actually exercised in production\n- [ ] the dormancy notes in `inbound_probe.py`, `SupportsAbsenceProbe` and ADR 0028 are\n      updated or removed to match the outcome","id":"f020-0960-2c42-4d61","parent_id":"","priority":2,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"task","title":"Decide the fate of the dormant absence-probe port (probe_remote / SupportsAbsenceProbe / inbound_probe.py)"},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1785877325739850001,"uuid":"3561c880-7934-44fd-8046-9f6eddb14f26"}